  TS["trust-supply-chain"]={t:"Software supply-chain security",h:"What goes into Semester, and how we keep it honest.",lede:"The controls on our code, dependencies and build pipeline today, what's planned next, and the evidence you can ask for. Every “in place” line below is checked against the product repository.",access:"Public",
    html:`<div class="tbl-wrap"><table><caption>In place today</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Control</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">Status</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Lockfiles</th><td>Every package in the repository has a lockfile, and CI installs exactly what it records (npm ci).</td><td><span class="status st-av">In place</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">CI on every pull request</th><td>Typecheck, lint, the test suite in three configurations, a build, smoke and accessibility journeys and data validation run on every pull request and every merge to main.</td><td><span class="status st-av">In place</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Secret scanning</th><td>gitleaks scans each change and every file in the branch, with a reviewed, value-scoped allowlist.</td><td><span class="status st-av">In place</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Dependency advisories</th><td>npm audit reports high and critical advisories on every CI run. It is non-blocking by design, so an overnight advisory can't stall unrelated work.</td><td><span class="status st-av">In place</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Dependency updates</th><td>Dependabot opens weekly update pull requests for the app's packages and for the CI actions.</td><td><span class="status st-av">In place</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Key rotation runbook</th><td>A written process lists every secret, what it grants and how to revoke it.</td><td><span class="status st-av">In place</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Pinned CI actions</th><td>Every third-party GitHub Action is pinned to an immutable full commit hash, with the readable release version kept in a comment.</td><td><span class="status st-av">In place</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">SBOM per release</th><td>The Pages release creates a CycloneDX inventory from the exact production lockfile and retains it with the run for 90 days.</td><td><span class="status st-av">In place</span></td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Branch protection and org ownership</th><td>A ruleset for main (no force push, no deletion, reviews recorded) is written into the repository and awaits the owner\'s import in GitHub settings, so it isn\'t enforced yet. The repository still lives under a personal account.</td><td><span class="status st-pl">Planned</span></td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <div class="tbl-wrap" data-site-style="seee3f850c9f4"><table><caption>Planned next</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Capability</th><th scope="col">What it will do</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Build provenance and signing</th><td>Each release tied to its commit, lockfile, test run and reviewer, signed where tooling supports it.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Dependency approval registry</th><td>A new production dependency states its purpose, data and runtime impact, and gets a license, security and provenance review.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Supplier-compromise runbook</th><td>Impact assessment, patch or disable, customer notice, and a tabletop exercise.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">License inventory</th><td>Open-source licenses tracked, with prohibited categories named.</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <div class="tbl-wrap" data-site-style="seee3f850c9f4"><table class="tight"><caption>Severity and patch policy (target, planned)</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Severity</th><th scope="col">Example</th><th scope="col">Target response</th></tr></thead><tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Critical</th><td>Active exploit, remote code execution, exposed secret, supply-chain compromise</td><td>Immediate assessment; mitigate, patch or disable</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">High</th><td>Serious auth, data or infrastructure flaw with a credible path</td><td>Prioritized patch; compensating control if delayed</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Medium</th><td>Constrained conditions or limited impact</td><td>Scheduled by risk and criticality</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Low</th><td>Minimal impact or non-production scope</td><td>Normal maintenance</td></tr>
    </tbody></table></div>
    <div class="grid-2" data-site-style="s0e06d6c53944"><div class="box"><p class="eyebrow">Evidence you can request now</p><ul class="check" data-site-style="s0ee7f66ad9c0"><li>CI workflow definitions and recent run history</li><li>Lockfiles and dependency audit output</li><li>Dependabot update history</li><li>Secret-scanning configuration and its allowlist rationale</li><li>The key rotation and incident runbook</li></ul></div>
      <div class="box"><p class="eyebrow">Evidence once built</p><ul class="check" data-site-style="s0ee7f66ad9c0"><li>Build provenance records</li><li>Source-control access reviews</li><li>Supplier-compromise tabletop report</li><li>Third-party component risk register</li></ul></div></div>`,secs:[]};
